#[doc("""
What a run says about the files a kalku would not search.

A kalku reports a file it could not read or parse in `skipped` rather than
failing the request, so one bad file does not cost the others. That is only
honest if the run then says so: a run whose every file was skipped has
nothing to measure, and "0 sites found" with no reason reads as "there was
nothing to break".
""")]

import kalku.wire.shapes

#[doc("One skipped file, in a line a person can act on.")]
pub fn skipped_line(s: Skipped) : String = "#{s.file}: #{s.reason}: #{s.message}"

#[doc("""
Why a run cannot go on, when the kalku looked at the files it was given and
could use none of them; `None` when at least one yielded a site, or when no
file was skipped (a project with nothing to mutate is not an error).
""")]
pub fn nothing_usable(found: Int, skipped: [Skipped]) : Option[String] =
  if found == 0 and not is_empty(skipped) {
    Some(
      "no file could be searched for sites:\n#{join(skipped | (s => "  #{skipped_line(s)}"), "\n")}"
    )
  } else {
    None
  }
